---
import SightlineLayout from "@/layouts/SightlineLayout.astro";
---

<SightlineLayout
  title="About — Sightline"
  description="How Sightline estimates camera positions, checks the results, and shows them in a 3D map."
>
  <article class="about-page sightline-shell">
    <header class="sightline-page-head">
      <h1>About Sightline</h1>
      <p>
        Sightline shows photographs from San Francisco and Toronto alongside
        estimates of where the camera was and what it could see. Reconstruction
        runs offline before reviewed results are added to the site. Opening a
        city loads those existing results; the browser does not solve a new
        camera position.
      </p>
    </header>
    <section class="method-copy">
      <h2>Starting with camera data</h2>
      <p>
        The default method starts with the photograph’s GPS, compass direction,
        and focal length where available. These are imperfect clues. The next
        step is to find nearby Street View images and match visual features
        between them and the photograph. It then connects matched pixels to
        positions in the provider’s 3D scene. A camera solver uses those
        matching points to estimate position, direction, and field of view—the
        angle covered by the image.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="method-copy">
      <h2>Trying it without metadata</h2>
      <p>
        San Francisco also has an image-only experiment for a fixed set of
        photographs within a small, predefined area. It does not use embedded
        GPS, heading, or lens data to locate the image. Models rank possible
        places and reference views, then feature matching and the camera solver
        test the geometry. The result is fixed before device metadata is opened
        for comparison. Those differences are measurements against the device’s
        estimate, not surveyed ground truth.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="method-copy">
      <h2>Checking the result</h2>
      <p>
        Finding a camera solution is only one step. The verification process
        renders the proposed viewpoint and compares it with the original
        photograph. It checks the alignment, the spread of useful matches, and
        whether the scene depth supports the camera geometry. A repaired camera
        must be rendered and checked again.
      </p>
      <p>
        A solved pose still needs verification and publication approval. The
        labels tell you whether a result was accepted, rejected, approximate, or
        still missing information. Rejection can mean weak matches or missing
        verification evidence. GPS error, changed buildings, and gaps in the 3D
        model can all prevent a reliable alignment.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="method-copy">
      <h2>Using the viewer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Show frustum</strong> draws the camera’s estimated field of view.
        <strong>Align camera</strong> moves the map to that viewpoint.
        <strong>Compare photo</strong> overlays the photograph so you can check how
        well it lines up. The available controls depend on the data approved for each
        photo. An overlay can look convincing even when the estimated position or
        angle is wrong.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="method-copy">
      <h2>What is public</h2>
      <p>
        The public site contains resized photos and camera data approved for
        display. Original files, full EXIF metadata, and detailed run evidence
        stay private. The viewer requests map imagery directly from its
        provider, keeps attribution visible, and does not store that imagery as
        site content.
      </p>
    </section>
    <a class="sightline-text-link" href="/"
      >Back to the collection <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
    >
  </article>
</SightlineLayout>
